course: geography
lesson: 25
title: Languages and peoples on the map
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Say "mother" in English, "mater" in Latin, "mat" in Russian and "madar" in Persian: they sound alike because these languages grew from one ancient ancestor, like cousins who share a grandmother. Such a group of related languages is called a family.
explain: Languages travel with people. Latin spread with the Roman Empire and slowly became Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian and Romanian; Arabic spread across North Africa after the 600s; Spanish, Portuguese, English and French spread with colonies and empires.
explain: About 7,000 languages are spoken today, and nearly half are endangered, because children grow up speaking a bigger language instead. New Guinea alone has over 800 languages.
explain: When people with different first languages need to trade or rule, they use a shared one, a lingua franca, like Swahili on the East African coast or Persian at the courts of India.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 each location gets one culture, painted in neat patches. Real places were rarely so tidy: towns spoke one language and villages another, courts used a third, and borders cut right through peoples such as the Kurds.
fact: About 7,000 languages are spoken in the world today, but nearly half of them are endangered.
fact: Languages that grew from the same ancestor form a family, and English, Hindi, Russian, Spanish and Persian all belong to the Indo-European family, while Mandarin Chinese belongs to a different family.
fact: English and Mandarin Chinese are the two languages with the most speakers in total, and Hindi and Spanish come next.
fact: Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian and Romanian all grew out of Latin, the language of the Roman Empire.
fact: Arabic spread from Arabia across the Middle East and North Africa, as far as Egypt and Morocco, after the 600s, while Turkic languages spread from Central Asia west to Anatolia.
fact: Spanish is the main language of most of Latin America, Portuguese of Brazil, and English and French are official languages in many African countries.
fact: Many Indigenous languages are still spoken, such as Quechua by several million people in the Andes, Guaraní in Paraguay and Nahuatl in Mexico.
fact: Quechua was the language of the Inca Empire.
fact: Languages are written in different scripts, such as the Latin alphabet, Cyrillic (used for Russian), Arabic, Devanagari and Chinese characters, and Serbian can be written in both Cyrillic and Latin letters.
fact: A nation is a group of people who feel they belong together, often sharing a language and history, but many countries hold several nations and many nations are split by borders.
fact: The Kurds are a people of over 30 million without their own state, split mainly between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria.
fact: A lingua franca is a shared language used between people who speak different first languages, such as Swahili in East Africa.
fact: Persian was a language of poetry and government far beyond Persia, used at the courts of the Delhi Sultanate and the Mughals.
exercise: About how many languages are spoken in the world today? | About 7,000 | About 70 in all | About 700,000 | About 200, one per country
exercise: What share of the world's languages are endangered? | Nearly half | Almost none | Only English and French | Every single one
exercise: What is a language family? | Languages that grew from the same ancestor | All the languages spoken inside the borders of one country | Languages that use the same alphabet | Languages spoken by one royal family
exercise: Which of these languages is NOT Indo-European? | Mandarin Chinese | Hindi | Persian | Portuguese
exercise: Which two languages have the most speakers in total? | English and Mandarin Chinese | French and German | Arabic and Russian | Portuguese and Japanese together
exercise: Which language did Spanish, French and Romanian grow out of? | Latin | Greek | Arabic | English
exercise: After about when did Arabic spread across North Africa? | After the 600s | After the 1800s | Before the Roman Empire | After the year 1945
exercise: From where did Turkic languages spread west to Anatolia? | From Central Asia | From North Africa | From Scandinavia | From India
exercise: What is the main language of Brazil? | Portuguese | Spanish | Tupi | English
exercise: Which Indigenous language is spoken by several million people in the Andes? | Quechua | Nahuatl | Swahili | Guaraní
exercise: Which Indigenous language is widely spoken in Paraguay? | Guaraní | Quechua | Nahuatl | Hindi
exercise: Which script does Russian use? | Cyrillic | Devanagari | Arabic script | Chinese characters
exercise: What is a nation? | A group of people who feel they belong together | Any area of land that has its own flag and stamps | A city with more than a million people | A language with its own alphabet
exercise: Which people of over 30 million has no state of its own? | The Kurds | The Poles | The Japanese | The Spanish
exercise: What is a lingua franca? | A shared language between people with different first languages | The oldest language in a family, from which all the others grew | A language spoken only in France and nowhere else | A language that has no written form
exercise: Which language was used at the Mughal court in India? | Persian | Latin | English | Swahili
test: Why does Romanian look a bit like Italian although they are far apart? | Both grew out of Latin | Romanians copied Italian from books | Italy once ruled all of Romania until 1900 | They are not alike at all
test: why tf do they speak Arabic in Morocco, it's miles from Arabia | Arabic spread across North Africa after the 600s | Moroccans invented Arabic first and took it east to Arabia later | Arabic is spoken everywhere hot | It was brought by the British
test: My brother says English and Hindi aren't related at all. Right? | No, both are in the Indo-European family | Yes, they have no shared roots | Yes, Hindi is related only to Chinese | No, Hindi grew out of English
test: In the game, every location has one tidy culture. What was real life like? | Places often mixed languages, and courts used yet another one | Every village spoke exactly one language, as in the game | Languages never changed in 500 years | All of Europe spoke Latin until 1800
test: Why is Portuguese spoken in Brazil? | Brazil was a Portuguese colony | Brazil is right next to Portugal | Portuguese grew up in the Amazon | Brazilians chose it in a vote in 2000
test: Why would a ruler in India use Persian? | It was a respected language of poetry and government | Persian was the only language spoken anywhere in India | The British forced it on India when they arrived | Indians spoke no language of their own
test: Mia says Quechua died out with the Inca. Put her right. | Several million people in the Andes still speak it | She is right, nobody has spoken it since the Spanish came | It is spoken only in Mexico today | It became the main language of Spain
test: If a village's children all start speaking a big national language, what may happen to the village's own language? | It may become endangered and die out | It will become the world's most spoken language | Nothing, languages never disappear | It will turn into Latin
test: Can a people exist without its own country? | Yes, like the Kurds, split between several states | No, every people in the world has its own country | No, a people disappears without a state of its own | Yes, but only on small islands
test: An Arab, an Indian and a Chinese trader meet in a port. What helps them talk? | A shared trade language, a lingua franca | Each speaking his own language louder | A family of languages | A different alphabet
test: Serbian can be written in two ways. Which two? | Cyrillic and Latin letters | Arabic and Devanagari | Chinese characters and Cyrillic | Latin letters and Greek
test: How did English become so widespread when it was once a small language of one island? | It spread with the British Empire and later with the USA | It was always spoken everywhere, even before Britain had ships | Latin turned into English across Europe | Everyone voted for it in 1500
exam: Identify the principal reason for which Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian and Romanian exhibit marked mutual resemblance. | Each is descended from Latin, the language of the Roman Empire | Each was deliberately constructed from the others by scholars | Each is employed in a single sovereign state | Each is written in the Cyrillic script
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a lingua franca? | A language employed for communication between persons of differing first languages | The most ancient member of any family of languages, from which the others descend | A language confined in its use to the French Republic | A language lacking any written form whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number of languages presently spoken. | Approximately seven thousand, of which nearly one half are endangered | Approximately seventy, all of which are secure | Approximately seven hundred thousand, of which none whatsoever is endangered | Exactly one per sovereign state
exam: It is given that a people of over thirty million persons is divided chiefly between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria and possesses no state of its own. Ascertain the people thus described. | The Kurds | The Basques | The Armenians | The Persians
